{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1794234598,"nonce":"vBQkWowRL9yRsDf8cPW3C8ShMpg=","title":"The code editor's caret paints as a line-wide block after a panel beside it closes and opens","message":"Reported on the russl toy (macOS, windowed): with the caret in the main\neditor, closing and reopening the inspector sidebar leaves a caret-coloured\nblock the width of the code area and the height of a line on the caret's\nline, in place of the 2 px caret.\n\nNot reproduced headless: `Headless` after the same clicks shows the caret\ncanvas laid out 2 x 17 px and painted as such. The block has the geometry of\nthe active-line highlight (`.blinc-code-active-line`, absolute, `w_full`,\nline height) and the caret's colour, which points at the windowed\ncompositor's canvas replay between full paints\n(`blinc_app::context::canvas_replay`): it re-runs each `CanvasPaintRecord`'s\n`render_fn` and splices its primitives into the cached batch at the record's\n`primitive_range`. If the editor's subtree is rebuilt (the editor is a\nStateful that rebuilds its content) or relaid out by the panel change\nwithout a full paint, a record's range or `bounds_wh` can belong to another\nnode; the caret callback fills `bounds_wh`, so stale bounds would paint\nexactly this. Headless does not run that replay path.\n\nNext step: reproduce through `BlincApp` with the compositor (as\n`context/tests/retained_canvas_tests.rs` does) by toggling a sibling panel's\nvisibility with a focused code editor, then check the record's bounds and\nrange against the batch.","files":null}]}